! jb_ax5b "I-Box 5", routine 1268 -- the ROUTINE'S TAIL AND CONSTANT
! POOL (PDF p.111, octal 024376.00-024411.00, 21 rows) and the
! ROUTINE'S ENTRY (PDF p.110, octal 024333.00-024335.40, 6 rows plus
! one silent CNOP and one location-only ident row).  Transcribed
! VERBATIM at the listing's own addresses and checked bit-exactly by
! verify_ax5b_1268_tail_pool_listing.py.
!
! ====================================================================
! PREMISE CHECK FIRST.  Routine 1268 runs 024333.00-024411.00 over
! PDF pp.110-111.  Three committed files cover parts of it:
!   verify_ax5b_1681_listing.py         024337.00-024344.00 (test 1A)
!   verify_ax5b_1268_test1bc_listing.py 024344.40-024363.00 (1B/1C)
!   verify_ax5b_1683_listing.py         024363.40-024375.40 (test 2)
! NOT ONE of the addresses transcribed here appears in ANY of their
! ROWS lists.  Where they do appear is worth naming precisely,
! because a grep alone would mislead:
!   * 024405.00 / 024407.00 / 024410.00 / 024411.00 appear in those
!     files' DOCSTRINGS and in their SYN blocks, as INPUTS
!     (I68K1, I68K2, I68K3, IC268).
!   * 024405.00 / 024407.00 / 024410.00 appear in
!     programs/ax5b_1268_test1bc.strap as PLACED SUBSTITUTIONS --
!     labels CREG, K1, K2, written there solely "so the block could
!     be run", after that file's own SLCs.  They are not in its ROWS
!     and nothing checks them.
!   * 024376.00 appears in programs/ax5b_1683_df_close_to_ds.strap
!     only as the address of that file's substituted "BEW,$" exit.
!   * 024404.00 and 024411.00 appear in ax5b_1274_truncation's prose.
!   * 024333.00-024336.00 appear only in prose in
!     verify_ax5b_162_test3b_listing.py and ax5b_1268_test1bc.strap.
! Both ranges below are new ROWS coverage.
!
! ====================================================================
! WHAT THE TAIL DOES, AND THE ONE RESULT WORTH THE ROW COUNT.
!
! The tail is the deck's standard routine epilogue: loop once, return
! to SSIP, update the continuity register with TEST 2's bit, then
! COMPARE the continuity register against its expected constant and
! report "-CONTINUITY ERROR." if it differs, then fall through into
! routine 1274.  The pool that follows holds the continuity register
! IC268, its expected value ICK268, and the three planted words
! I68K1/I68K2/I68K3 that tests 1A/1B/1C and 2A use.
!
! ICK268 PRINTS 600000 OCTAL IN BITS 0-17.  Read off its own row at
! 024406.00, which prints "600000.00 00 000000.00 00".  600000 octal
! = 110 000 000 000 000 000 binary = BITS 0 AND 1 SET, and nothing
! else in the whole 64-bit word.  THE ROUTINE THEREFORE CLAIMS
! EXACTLY TWO SUB-TESTS.
!
! That is NOT a discrepancy with the six sub-tests the deck names
! (1A, 1B, 1C, 2A, 2B, 2C), and this file does not report it as one.
! The continuity register counts "V+" UPDATES, not sub-tests, and
! routine 1268 performs exactly two of them, both now transcribed:
!     024362.40  V+,$X13,BIT0   24522.32 B0   (verify_..._test1bc)
!     024401.00  V+,$X13,BIT1   24523.32 B0   (this file)
! The routine's own banner on p.110 says the same thing in words:
! "-THIS TEST IS COMPOSED OF TWO ROUTINES".  1A/1B/1C are the three
! memories TEST 1 sweeps and they share bit 0; 2A/2B/2C are the three
! memories TEST 2 sweeps and they share bit 1.  So this block closes
! a loop across three rows: the two bit numbers the routine sets, and
! the constant it then checks them against, all agree.
!
! The comparison against 1260 and 1258 that the project has drawn
! twice before holds and is sharpened.  ICK260 = 777774 = bits 0-15 =
! sixteen, and routine 1260 genuinely has sixteen NUMBERED sub-tests.
! ICK258 = 600000 = two, for a routine with two tests each having
! sub-parts -- structurally the same shape as 1268.  The constant
! counts continuity BITS, and bits map to tests, not to sub-tests.
!
! ====================================================================
! BRANCH POLARITY IN THESE ROWS: THE MINORITY SHAPE.  "SIC,SEN"
! comes FIRST and the conditional branch goes TO "SERS":
!     024403.00  SIC,SEN        1310.00 80
!     024403.40  BZXE,SERS      1304.32 C0   -CONTINUITY ERROR.
! Read out of the printed bits, not out of prose.  Reference Manual
! printed p.40 (PDF p.43), BRANCH ON INDICATOR: "This operation
! causes the specified indicator to be tested.  If its value matches
! that of bit 30 of the instruction, bits 0-18 of the effective
! address replace the instruction counter contents."  Hex C0 =
! 11000000, so bit 30 = 0: the branch is taken when XE is OFF.  The
! preceding "KV,$X13,ICK268" sets XE on EQUAL, so XE OFF means the
! continuity register did NOT match -- and the branch target is the
! error reporter, with the message naming the anomaly.  The
! indicator number is bits 19-24 = 110101 = 53 = XE.  Contrast
! BXVZ,SERS elsewhere in this routine, hex 42 = 01000010, bit 30 = 1.
! Both shapes appear in this deck; THIS block uses the SIC-first,
! branch-to-SERS shape, the same as routine 1274 and as 1268's own
! 1B/1C rows.
! Reference Manual printed p.41 (PDF p.44), STORE INSTRUCTION COUNTER
! IF: "This instruction may always be prefixed to one of the
! half-word branching instructions.  It is executed if and only if
! the associated branching is successful."
!
! ====================================================================
! HEADINGS, ANNOTATIONS AND ERROR MESSAGES, VERBATIM, WITH OCTAL
! LOCATIONS.  Exactly ONE of these is an error message.
!
!   ERROR MESSAGE (printed beside the branch that reports it):
!     024403.40  -CONTINUITY ERROR.
!
!   ANNOTATIONS (not error messages -- they label the rows they sit
!   beside and no branch reports them):
!     024377.40  -TO SSIP
!     024400.40  -UPDATE CONTINUITY CHECK.
!     024405.00  -CONTINUITY REG 1268.
!     024333.00  -UPDATE IDENT.
!     024334.40  -PRINT IDENT.
!
!   "-TO SSIP" AT 024377.40 CARRIES NO PERIOD.  Re-measured here from
!   the p.111 crop at 500 dpi rather than inherited: the comment ends
!   with the P of SSIP and the next ink on that row is 3000 px away
!   in the machine-code column.  024361.00's "-TO SSIP." on p.110,
!   the identical row one loop earlier, DOES carry one.  This
!   independently corroborates the contrast
!   verify_ax5b_1268_test1bc_listing.py flagged from the other side.
!
!   THE ROUTINE'S BANNER is on p.110 above 024333.00 and is quoted in
!   full, with its measured dash counts, in
!   programs/ax5b_1268_test1bc.strap.  It is not re-quoted here and
!   nothing in that file is edited.
!
! ====================================================================
! BIT-18 SELF-CHECKS.  Print format: bits 0-17 as 6 octal digits, ".",
! bits 18-23 as 2 OCTAL digits, then bits 24-31 as 2 HEX digits.  Bit
! 18 is the TOP bit of the octal pair.  THREE classes of operand
! appear in these rows and only ONE of them puts a half-word select
! there.  Which class an operand is in comes from the Reference
! Manual, not from the listing.
!
! (a) BRANCH TARGETS -- bit 18 IS the half-word select.  CHECKED.
!     Reference Manual printed p.39 (PDF p.42): "The address field,
!     bits 0-18, represents the branch address.  Unconditional
!     branching is specified by bits 19-27, where bits 19-21, the OP
!     field, select the particular operation.  Bits 28-31 name the
!     index register used for address modification."  And on the
!     instruction counter, same page: "When the nineteenth bit is a
!     one, the full word taken from storage will start at bit 32 of
!     the addressed location ...  When the nineteenth bit is a zero,
!     the full word will start at bit 0" -- the nineteenth bit is
!     bit 18.
!
!     loc        source        printed     pair    b18  -> target
!     024376.00  B,$+1.0       24377.10 00  001000  0   024377.00
!     024376.40  BD,I683       24363.44 00  100100  1   024363.40
!     024377.40  B,SSW         1301.10 00   001000  0   001301.00
!     024400.00  BD,$+.32      24400.44 00  100100  1   024400.40
!     024403.40  BZXE,SERS     1304.32 C0   011010  0   001304.00
!     024404.00  BD,I74        24412.04 00  000100  0   024412.00
!     024334.40  B,IDF1        1443.10 00   001000  0   001443.00
!     024335.40  BD,I681       24337.04 00  000100  0   024337.00
!
!     Read back against the page's own label column: p.111 prints
!     I683's row at 024363.40 -- an ODD half-word, and its pair's bit
!     18 is 1.  p.110 prints I681's row at 024337.00 -- an EVEN
!     half-word, and its pair's bit 18 is 0.  024412.00 is routine
!     1274's first row (PDF p.112, its own SLC address).  The
!     self-relative pair carries the same information: "B,$+1.0" at
!     024376.00 targets 024377.00 with bit 18 = 0, while the SAME
!     source text at 024357.40 in the 1B/1C block targets 024360.40
!     and prints bit 18 = 1.  Bits 19-23 are constant per mnemonic
!     (01000 for B, 00100 for BD), so bit 18 is the ONLY bit that
!     moves with the target's half-word parity.  That is what makes
!     this a check rather than a restatement.
!
! (b) LX AND SX -- bit 18 is NOT a half-word select.  EXCLUDED, with
!     the manual's reason.  Reference Manual printed p.23 (PDF p.26),
!     "Direct Index Arithmetic": the format's tick marks read ADDRESS
!     0, J 19, OP. 23, I 28, 31, and the text says "In the majority
!     of these operations, 19 bits of the effective address are used
!     in order to permit addressing of half-words.  THE TWO
!     EXCEPTIONS USE ONLY 18 BITS OF THE EFFECTIVE ADDRESS BECAUSE
!     THEY REQUIRE FULL-WORD OPERANDS."  Printed p.23: "Load Index
!     (LX).  The index word specified by J is replaced by the
!     full-word specified by bits 0-17 of the effective address."
!     Printed p.24 (PDF p.27): "Store Index (SX).  The index word
!     specified by J is stored in the full-word memory location
!     specified by bits 0-17 of the effective address."  And the
!     programming note that closes the section: "The LOAD INDEX and
!     STORE INDEX operations involve transmission of full words.  In
!     all other operations the effective operand address refers to a
!     half-word."  LX and SX ARE the two exceptions.  Their bit 18 is
!     a low-order address bit forced to zero.
!
!     What their pairs DO carry, checked instead: bit 18 = 0, bits
!     19-22 = J, bit 23 = the top bit of the 5-bit op code.
!       024400.40  LX,$X13,IC268   24405.32 10   0|1101|0  J=13, LX
!       024401.40  SX,$X13,IC268   24405.33 10   0|1101|1  J=13, SX
!       024402.00  LX,$X13,IC268   24405.32 10   same as the first
!       024333.00  LX,$X1,I68ID    24336.02 10   0|0001|0  J=1,  LX
!       024333.40  SX,$X1,DPET13   1437.03 10    0|0001|1  J=1,  SX
!     The 32/33 contrast on two rows naming the SAME symbol isolates
!     bit 23 exactly; DIR_INDEX_CODES["LX"]=0b00001 and ["SX"]=
!     0b10001 differ in that bit alone.  This corroborates the note
!     verify_ax5b_1268_test1bc_listing.py makes about pair 33 vs 32.
!     The J placement is falsified by the digits themselves: if J sat
!     at bits 18-21, $X13 = 1101 would make the pair start with 1 and
!     print 64-77 octal, not 32.
!
! (c) V+ AND KV -- bit 18 IS a half-word select, and it reads 0.
!     CHECKED.  These are NOT the two exceptions.  Reference Manual
!     printed p.24 (PDF p.27): "Add to Value (V+).  The address part,
!     bits 0-24, of the half-word addressed by bits 0-18 of the
!     effective address is added to the address part, bits 0-24, of
!     the index word specified by J."  And: "Compare Value (KV).  The
!     address part, bits 0-24, of the half-word addressed by bits
!     0-18 of the effective address is compared with the address
!     part, bits 0-24, of the index word specified by J."
!       024401.00  V+,$X13,BIT1    24523.32 B0   b18=0 -> 024523.00
!       024402.40  KV,$X13,ICK268  24406.32 90   b18=0 -> 024406.00
!     Read back: p.111's label column prints ICK268 at 024406.00, an
!     EVEN half-word, and bit 18 is 0.  BIT1's own row is on p.114
!     and is NOT on either of these pages -- see THE BIT TABLE below.
!
! (d) SIC -- EXCLUDED, and this is the trap the print format sets.
!     Reference Manual printed p.41 (PDF p.44), "Storage of
!     Instruction Counter": "Bits 0-23 constitute the storage
!     address.  Bits 24-27, together with the half-word branch
!     instruction operation code, specify the full-word operation.
!     Bits 28-31 name the index register used for storage address
!     modification.  Bits 32-63 specify the half-word branch
!     instruction used."  The diagram's tick marks read ADDRESS 0 ..
!     18 .. 24, 1000, 28 I, 32.  So SIC's operand is a 24-BIT BIT
!     ADDRESS and bits 18-23 are a BIT OFFSET WITHIN THE WORD, not a
!     half-word select.
!       024377.00  SIC,SEN0+.32   1311.40 80
!       024403.00  SIC,SEN        1310.00 80
!       024334.00  SIC,RET        1306.40 80
!     THE DIGITS CANNOT TELL THE TWO READINGS APART, because 32 bits
!     is also exactly one half-word: pair 40 octal = 32 decimal, and
!     a "bit 18 = 1" reading would also say ".40".  THE ARITHMETIC
!     SETTLES IT.  The source of the first row writes "+.32" -- a
!     count of 32 BITS -- and the pair is 40 octal = 32 decimal, the
!     whole 6-bit field carrying the number.  Under a half-word
!     reading only bit 18 would be used and bits 19-23 would have to
!     mean something else; the manual gives them nothing else to
!     mean, because it assigns bits 0-23 entire to the address and
!     starts the next field at 24.  The 1306.40 of "SIC,RET" is the
!     same field doing the same job with no "+.32" written at all:
!     RET is itself the bit address 001306.40 (PDF p.116's SYN table
!     prints "RET SYN,%8@1306.40").
!
! ====================================================================
! WITNESS TIERING.  Every SYN below is an INPUT, never a witness: its
! value was read off a printed column and handed to the assembler, so
! a row naming it can only give that number back.  A label placed by
! an SLC is an ANCHOR, not evidence, for the same reason.
!
! GENUINELY WITNESSED -- ALL FIVE POOL ADDRESSES.  There is ONE SLC
! for the whole tail, at 024376.0, and the block runs unbroken to
! 024411.00.  Every pool label's value therefore falls out of the
! assembler's own size accounting from that single anchor:
!
!     024376.0 + 14 half-words          = 024404.40
!     CNOP rounds up (one NOP emitted)  = 024405.00   IC268
!     + one full word                   = 024406.00   ICK268
!     + one full word                   = 024407.00   I68K1
!     + two half-words                  = 024410.00   I68K2
!     + two half-words                  = 024411.00   I68K3
!
! and each is then checked against TWO independent printed columns:
! the LOCATION column on the right of p.111 (the SYMBOLS assertions
! in the verify file), and, for IC268 and ICK268, the MACHINE-CODE
! column of rows that name them (24405 on four rows, 24406 on one).
! A sixth, cross-PAGE check falls out of the entry block: p.110's
! "Z,IC268" at 024335.00 prints "24405.22 00", so a DIFFERENT PAGE's
! machine code corroborates the address this file derived on p.111.
!
! This converts five addresses that all three existing 1268 files
! take as SYN'd inputs into witnessed ones.
!
! ANCHORS, not witnesses: L1268T (this file's own name for the tail's
! first row -- the listing's label column is EMPTY for all fourteen
! tail rows) and I68 at 024333.00 (a real printed label, but placed
! by this file's second SLC).  Neither is asserted.
!
! NOT WITNESSED, and named so it is not mistaken for one: I68ID at
! 024336.00.  The listing prints a LOCATION for that row and NO
! MACHINE CODE (see below), so it is SYN'd, from the LOCATION column
! -- deliberately not from "LX,$X1,I68ID"'s machine code, which would
! make the 024333.00 row a tautology.  Taken that way, the 24336 in
! that row's machine code is a real cross-check of one printed column
! against the other.
!
! ====================================================================
! THE BIT TABLE, stated precisely rather than claimed.  The rule is
! BIT<n> = the word at octal 024522 + n.  "V+,$X13,BIT1" at
! 024401.00 prints "24523.32 B0", i.e. the assembler resolved BIT1 to
! word 024523 = 024522 + 1.
!
! THIS FILE DOES NOT WITNESS THE TABLE.  BIT1 is SYN'd here, and the
! value of the SYN comes from this very row's machine code, so the
! check re-derives its own input.  What the row IS: a SECOND,
! INDEPENDENT INSTANCE of a value already recorded once in this
! repository -- programs/ax5b_1258_test2.strap line 162 carries
! "BIT1  SYN, (8)24523.0   ! from V+,$X13,BIT1  = 24523.32 B0",
! read off ROUTINE 1258 on PDF p.5.  Different routine, different
! page, same number.  That is corroboration, not a new witness.
!
! What WOULD witness the table is p.114's own label column, where
! BIT0..BIT35 are defined.  Grepped: NO verify_*.py in this
! repository has a ROWS entry on p.114's BIT table.  It is quoted in
! prose in ax5b_162_test1cc.strap ("BIT0 XW,%8@400000.00,0,0" at
! 024522.00), in ax5b_1258_test2.strap ("BIT1 XW,(8)200000.00,0,0")
! and in ax5b_1274_truncation.strap, and SYN'd in several files, but
! never checked.  It remains an open transcription target.
!
! ====================================================================
! CNOP, KEPT VERBATIM AND NEVER HAND-EXPANDED -- AND THE PAGE SHOWS
! BOTH OF ITS BRANCHES, one in each of this file's two ranges.
!
!   024404.40 (p.111): the CNOP row prints machine code "0.30 00" and
!   the location 024404.40.  The preceding "BD,174" sits at
!   024404.00, so the counter is mid-word and CNOP fills the gap with
!   a NOP before the full-word XW at 024405.00.
!
!   between 024335.40 and 024336.00 (p.110): the CNOP row prints
!   NEITHER A LOCATION NOR MACHINE CODE.  The preceding "BD,I681"
!   sits at 024335.40, the second half of word 024335, so the counter
!   is already on the word boundary 024336.00 and CNOP has nothing to
!   fill.
!
! WHICH OF THE TWO IS LOAD-BEARING WAS MEASURED, and the measurement
! corrected what this header first said.
!
!   THE 024404.40 CNOP IS LOAD-BEARING.  Deleting the "CNOP" line
!   below makes 024404.40 assemble as an all-zero half-word,
!   "000000.00 00" against the page's "000000.30 00" -- one observed
!   failure and nothing else.  The automatic gap filler does NOT
!   cover it: assemble() records a rounding gap only when the
!   statement that forced the rounding is an INSTRUCTION, and
!   strap_asm.py excludes XW from that test ("is_instruction =
!   stmt.op.upper() not in ('DD', 'VF', 'XW', 'END', 'SYN', 'SLC')").
!   The full-word XW at 024405.00 still ALIGNS the counter, which is
!   why the five pool addresses do not move -- but nothing fills the
!   half-word it skipped.  So this file WITNESSES CNOP's NOP
!   emission.  It does NOT witness CNOP's round-up, because XW's own
!   alignment would do that anyway.
!
!   THE p.110 CNOP IS NOT LOAD-BEARING.  Deleting it leaves ALL TESTS
!   PASSED.  Arithmetic reason, recorded rather than discarded: the
!   counter is already on a word boundary when it is reached, so
!   there is no gap to fill, and an SLC follows it, so nothing
!   downstream depends on where it left the counter.  The page's
!   silence is the evidence there; this file only fails to
!   contradict it.
!
! verify_ax5b_160_test8c_disabled_listing.py is the file that
! witnesses both CNOP branches from assembled output, and
! programs/ax5b_1258_test1.strap records the same caveat about a
! transcript that cuts at an SLC.
!
! ====================================================================
! WHERE THE LISTING PRINTS A LOCATION AND NO MACHINE CODE, NO WORD IS
! FABRICATED.  p.110's row at 024336.00 reads, in full:
!
!     I68ID   %IQSZ¤DD%BU,64,8¤,1268   Z            024336.00
!
! -- a label, a print macro whose expansion this assembler does not
! implement, and an empty machine-code column.  This file therefore
! stops the entry block at 024335.40 with the CNOP, SYNs I68ID from
! the printed LOCATION column, and places nothing at 024336.00.  The
! verify script asserts that word 024336 is ABSENT from the assembled
! image, so the "no fabrication" claim is checked rather than
! promised.  ax5b_1258_test1.strap and ax5b_1274_truncation.strap
! exclude the corresponding ident rows for the same reason; §7 of
! docs/listing_typography.md notes that had one been transcribed the
! digit-1 reading WOULD have become a real encoding error.
!
! ====================================================================
! FIRSTS FOR THIS REPO: NONE.  Every mnemonic in both ranges was
! grepped against every other programs/*.strap and verify_*.py before
! any claim was made, and every candidate was knocked out:
!   KV     27 .strap / 23 verify files      V+   26 / 23
!   BZXE   17 / 16                          CNOP 35 / 21
!   XW     58 / 43                          LVI  44 / 23
!   BD    117 / 76                          SIC 155 / 137
!   B, LX, SX, NOP, Z: ubiquitous.
! Nothing here is a first, and nothing is reported as one.
!
! ====================================================================
! GLYPHS.  docs/listing_typography.md settles by measurement that the
! leading glyph of these labels is the LETTER I (letter I 10-18 px at
! 600 dpi, digit 1 never below 24 px and 99% at 30+, with a flag and
! a base serif), so the page's true spellings are I68, I681, I683,
! I68ID, I68K1-3, IC268, ICK268, I74 and the banner "----I268---".
! That rename has since been made repo-wide, so the page's spellings
! and this file's are now the same thing.  THIS FILE FOLLOWS THE
! EXISTING 1268 FILES and starts no second convention: the measured
! letter-I names in the assembler source (I681, I683, I68K1, IC268,
! ...) and in the comments and the verify script's note column alike,
! exactly as ax5b_1681_if_close_to_ds.strap, ax5b_1268_test1bc.strap
! and ax5b_1683_df_close_to_ds.strap now do.  When this file was
! written those three carried the earlier misreading -- a digit read
! for the letter, with an "L" prefixed to make the name legal for this
! assembler -- and I74 at 024412.00 had to be called out as the ONE
! exception, because programs/ax5b_1274_truncation.strap already
! defined exactly that symbol at exactly that address ("SLC,
! (8)24412.0 / I74 LX,$X1,I74ID") and writing L174 would have invented
! a spelling no file uses.  It is no longer an exception; every 1268
! file spells the routine the way the page prints it.
! No new glyph measurement was made in this session.
!
! ====================================================================
! SUBSTITUTIONS, four, and a fifth that has been retired:
!   1. RETIRED.  This item used to read "numeric labels are prefixed
!      L, because this assembler's symbol pattern is
!      [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]* and reads a leading digit as an address".
!      The listing's own spellings -- I681, I683, I68K1, IC268, ... --
!      satisfy that pattern unaided once the leading glyph is read as
!      the letter it is, so there is no label substitution left; see
!      GLYPHS above and docs/listing_typography.md.
!   2. L1268T is this file's own name for the tail's first row.  The
!      listing prints NO label there; STRAP's END needs one.
!   3. SERS and SSW are "B,$" HANGS at their own printed addresses
!      001304.00 and 001301.00, and IDF1 likewise at 001443.00; the
!      real error-report, switch-scan and ident-print routines are
!      SSIP's and are not transcribed.  RET, SEN, SEN0 and DPET13
!      get one all-zero word each, as SIC's and SX's targets.
!   4. I68 falls straight through into the I681 block, which is
!      transcribed in ax5b_1681_if_close_to_ds.strap; a "BEW,$" exit
!      is placed at 024337.00 so this file terminates on its own.
!      Likewise the tail runs into routine 1274 at 024412.00, where a
!      second "BEW,$" stands in for ax5b_1274_truncation.strap.
!   5. BIT1W at 024523.00 is a PLACED word, quoted from p.114 via
!      ax5b_1258_test2.strap's transcription of it, so the block
!      could be run.  It is NOT transcribed from pp.110-111 (p.114
!      is a different page and is not read here) and it is NOT in
!      the verify script's ROWS.  Nothing checks it.
! Everything else is verbatim.
!
! NOT RUN.  This session was instructed not to invoke run_program.sh,
! run_tests.sh, bsc or mutation_score.py, so this file is an ENCODING
! oracle only and is NOT wired into run_tests.sh.  Nothing in this
! header claims anything about what the model does at run time.

! ---- externals: INPUTS to the check, never witnesses ---------------
! SSIP's entry points are from PDF p.116, this deck's own SYN table,
! which prints "SSW SYN,%8@1301.0 / SERS SYN,%8@1304.0 / RET
! SYN,%8@1306.40 / SEN SYN,%8@1310.0 / SEN0 SYN,%8@1311.0 / DPET13
! SYN,%8@1437.0 / IDF1 SYN,%8@1443.0".  Still inputs here.
SSW     SYN, (8)1301.0      ! p.116 SYN table
SERS    SYN, (8)1304.0      ! p.116 SYN table
RET     SYN, (8)1306.40     ! p.116 SYN table -- a BIT address
SEN     SYN, (8)1310.0      ! p.116 SYN table
SEN0    SYN, (8)1311.0      ! p.116 SYN table
DPET13  SYN, (8)1437.0      ! p.116 SYN table -- the ident cell
IDF1    SYN, (8)1443.0      ! p.116 SYN table -- "print ID" entry
! Deck labels outside both transcribed ranges.
I681   SYN, (8)24337.0     ! p.110 label column: I681, the loop head
I683   SYN, (8)24363.40    ! p.111 label column: I683, test 2's head
I68ID  SYN, (8)24336.0     ! p.110 LOCATION column; no machine code
I74     SYN, (8)24412.0     ! p.112: routine 1274's first row
BIT1    SYN, (8)24523.0     ! from V+,$X13,BIT1 = 24523.32 B0; the
                            ! same value ax5b_1258_test2.strap read
                            ! off routine 1258 on PDF p.5

! ====================================================================
! BLOCK A -- routine 1268's TAIL and CONSTANT POOL, PDF p.111,
! 024376.00-024411.00.  ONE SLC for the whole block: every pool
! address below is DERIVED from it, not placed.
! ====================================================================
        SLC, (8)24376.0
!
! ---- loop once, return to SSIP -------------------------------------
L1268T  B,   $+1.0                 !  skip the BD: -> 024377.00
        BD,  I683                 !  the loop back to I683
        SIC, SEN0+.32
        B,   SSW                   ! -TO SSIP      (no period, p.111)
        BD,  $+.32
!
! ---- the routine's continuity register, test 2's bit ---------------
        LX,  $X13, IC268          ! -UPDATE CONTINUITY CHECK.
        V+,  $X13, BIT1            !  test 2 done = bit 1
        SX,  $X13, IC268
!
! ---- the continuity CHECK ------------------------------------------
        LX,  $X13, IC268
        KV,  $X13, ICK268         !  against the expected word
        SIC, SEN
        BZXE, SERS                 ! -CONTINUITY ERROR.
!
! ---- fall through into routine 1274 --------------------------------
        BD,  I74
!
! ---- the constant pool ---------------------------------------------
        CNOP
IC268  XW,  0,0,0                 ! -CONTINUITY REG 1268.
ICK268 XW,  (8)600000.00,0,0      !  bits 0 AND 1: both tests done
!
I68K1  Z,   $X2                   !  the OLD instruction
        NOP
I68K2  LVI, $X2, (8)777777.40     !  the NEW one
        NOP
I68K3  XW,  0,0,0                 !  test 2A's scratch word

! ====================================================================
! BLOCK B -- routine 1268's ENTRY, PDF p.110, 024333.00-024335.40.
! Stops at the CNOP.  024336.00, the ident word, prints a LOCATION
! and NO MACHINE CODE and is NOT transcribed; I68ID is SYN'd above
! from that printed location.
! ====================================================================
        SLC, (8)24333.0
I68    LX,  $X1, I68ID           ! -UPDATE IDENT.
        SX,  $X1, DPET13
        SIC, RET
        B,   IDF1                  ! -PRINT IDENT.
        Z,   IC268                !  clear the continuity register
        BD,  I681
        CNOP                       !  prints NO location, NO code
!
! ---- substitution 4: the two exits ---------------------------------
        SLC, (8)24337.0
PASS1   BEW, $                     !  where I681's block begins
        SLC, (8)24412.0
PASS2   BEW, $                     !  where routine 1274 begins
!
! ---- substitution 3: SSIP stands in as hangs and scratch words -----
        SLC, (8)1301.0
SSWH    B, $
        SLC, (8)1304.0
SERSH   B, $
        SLC, (8)1306.0
RETW    DD(BU,64), 0               ! SIC,RET's target
        SLC, (8)1310.0
SENW    DD(BU,64), 0               ! SIC,SEN's target
        SLC, (8)1311.0
SEN0W   DD(BU,64), 0               ! SIC,SEN0+.32's target
        SLC, (8)1437.0
DPETW   DD(BU,64), 0               ! SX,$X1,DPET13's target
        SLC, (8)1443.0
IDF1H   B, $
!
! ---- substitution: the BIT table word this block names -------------
        SLC, (8)24523.0
BIT1W   XW,  (8)200000.00,0,0      ! quoted from p.114 in
                                   ! ax5b_1258_test2.strap; placed so
                                   ! the block could be run, NOT
                                   ! transcribed and NOT checked
        END, I68
